          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also called hypertension, means the pressure in your arteries stays raised over time, not just on one occasion. Blood pressure is written as two numbers: the top number is the pressure when the heart beats, and the bottom number is the pressure between beats. Both are measured in millimeters of mercury. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nOne high reading is not enough to call it high blood pressure. Pressure rises and falls through the day with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep, and the time of day. Because of this, a diagnosis is based on repeated measurements, and a single high reading in a clinic is not a diagnosis by itself. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n\nThe guidance also says the cutoff depends on where you live. The US guidance treats 130/80 mm Hg as the diagnostic threshold. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats readings from 130 to 139 over 80 to 89 as \"high-normal.\" The same reading can be labeled differently depending on which guidance is used. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, major cardiology society consensus guidance.)\n\nHigh blood pressure usually causes no symptoms, so it is most often found during routine measurement. If you have a reading you are worried about, a clinician can judge whether repeated readings are needed. The sources I searched did not cover what those readings should show or what to do next.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
